REFRIGERATION CYCLE 
============================================================

CONCEPTS
============================================================
• Compressor – Most expensive single component. Weak 
compression often shows as low discharge temperature and 
small temperature rise across the condenser.  

• Condenser coil – Air-cooled; performance is highly 
sensitive to dirt and ambient temperature.  

• Capillary tube – Fixed-orifice device. Restriction is 
diagnosed by a large temperature drop at the inlet and 
low suction pressure.  

• Evaporator coil – Cooling only happens here. Airflow is 
as important as refrigerant flow.  

• Thermostat / sensor – Modern units use NTC thermistors; 
resistance falls as temperature rises.  

• Refrigerant R600a – Hydrocarbon; flammable in high 
concentrations, so leak checks and proper ventilation 
matter. Charge is typically 40–60 g in small domestic 
units.  

• Failure-mode decision tree – Start with “Is the 
compressor running?” → “Is the condenser rejecting heat?” 
→ “Is the evaporator absorbing heat and moving air?” 
→ sealed system only if the first three are healthy.  

• Pressure/temperature table – For R600a the saturation 
temperature at a given pressure is the key reference 
(e.g., ~ –20 °C to –25 °C evaporating for a typical 
freezer).  

• Brand-specific symptom mapping – Varies (e.g., some 
Samsung/LG models have known damper or board issues; 
many Whirlpool/GE units are more classic capillary 
systems). Symptom → component mapping is best done 
with model-specific service data.

CORE VAPOR-COMPRESSION CYCLE
----------------------------
1. COMPRESSOR
   • Raises refrigerant pressure + temperature.
   • Output: hot, high-pressure vapor.
   • Enables heat rejection at condenser.

2. CONDENSER COIL
   • Rejects heat to room air.
   • Vapor → high-pressure liquid.
   • Head pressure reflects condenser health.

3. CAPILLARY TUBE (Expansion Device)
   • Restricts flow; drops pressure sharply.
   • Sets mass-flow rate.
   • Liquid → low-pressure mixture.

4. EVAPORATOR COIL
   • Refrigerant boils at low pressure.
   • Absorbs heat from cabinet interior.
   • Cooling occurs ONLY here.

5. THERMOSTAT / SENSOR
   • Cycles compressor to maintain set temperature.
   • Controls duty cycle.

6. REFRIGERANT (R600a)
   • Isobutane.
   • High efficiency, low GWP.
   • Very sensitive to charge amount.

